item.text:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, these are remote homologs, not merely unrelated proteins with a similar shape: all are in the all-alpha class a, Ferritin-like fold a.25, and Ferritin-like superfamily a.25.1. They separate at the family level into Ferritin a.25.1.1 (SUNID 47241), Ribonucleotide reductase-like a.25.1.2 (SUNID 47253), and Manganese catalase a.25.1.3 (SUNID 100951). Thus the shared superfamily supports probable common ancestry of the conserved helical core, whereas the three families represent substantial functional and active-site divergence. A shared fold alone would not establish homology, but their shared superfamily is the stronger evolutionary statement.

Their chemistries must not be transferred across those family boundaries. Ferritins primarily oxidize and mineralize iron for storage; RNR R2 proteins use a dinuclear metal/radical center to supply radical chemistry to the catalytic R1 subunit; manganese catalases use a Mn center to disproportionate hydrogen peroxide. Moreover, “Ribonucleotide reductase-like” is a structural-family label, not a universal function: SCOPe also places desaturases and monooxygenase subunits in a.25.1.2. Likewise, a.25.1.1 contains bacterioferritin and rubrerythrin-related proteins. Therefore neither substrate, metal identity, radical chemistry, reaction, nor oligomeric state can be assigned from superfamily membership alone.

A cross-family template may support modeling of the conserved core, but not reliable active-site geometry, metal/cofactor assignment, catalytic residues, insertions, interfaces, or exact boundaries. Representative selection should favor an experimentally characterized, high-quality structure from the same family and preferably the same biochemical subgroup; one ferritin is not representative of the whole superfamily, and automated matches should not be treated as functional standards. Chemistry-specific transfer requires external biochemical evidence such as activity assays plus metal/cofactor and oxidation-state characterization (for example spectroscopy), supported by active-site residue and structural analysis.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop — release-2.08 name discovery using /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ferritin&mode=auto&limit=50, /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ribonucleotide+reductase&mode=auto&limit=50, and /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=manganese+catalase&mode=auto&limit=50","mcp__scope__get_scop_node — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25/parents, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents, and the base, /parents, and /homology paths for /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241, /sunids/47253, and /sunids/100951","mcp__scope__list_scop_children — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/children?limit=50, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241/children?limit=50, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253/children?limit=50, and /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/children?limit=50","Pretrained biochemical knowledge for the distinct iron-storage, radical-generating, and peroxide-disproportionation chemistries and their required experimental validation"]}]}
